{
  "id": "konjugation",
  "name": "Key irregular verbs in the present",
  "level": "A1",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "subtitle": "Some very common French verbs do not follow the rules: aller, faire, venir, prendre, mettre, voir, dire. Learn them whole: je vais, je fais, je viens, je prends, je mets, je vois, je dis.",
  "briefMarks": ["aller", "faire", "venir", "prendre", "mettre", "voir", "dire"],
  "lookup": { "href": "/present-verbes-er", "label": "Regular verbs first? Present tense: -er verbs" },
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "je", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "vais · fais · viens · prends · mets · vois · dis", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Je vais</span> à la mairie. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Nous faisons</span> les courses. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Tu viens</span> demain<em> ?</em> · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Il prend</span> le bus. · <span class=\"vp-verb\">Je dis</span> bonjour.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>aller</strong> says where you go: <em>Je vais à Lyon. Nous allons chez le médecin.</em> It also asks and answers “how are you”: <em>Comment allez-vous ? – Je vais bien.</em>",
        "tag": "aller"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>faire</strong> means do or make: <em>Je fais la cuisine. Nous faisons les courses.</em> Weather too: <em>Il fait froid.</em> <em>Nous faisons</em> sounds like “nous fezons”.",
        "tag": "faire"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>venir, prendre, mettre</strong> share a pattern. The singular has no vowel ending, the plural is longer: <em>je viens, nous venons, ils viennent</em>; <em>je prends, nous prenons, ils prennent</em>; <em>je mets, nous mettons, ils mettent</em>.",
        "tag": "venir, prendre, mettre"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>voir</strong> and <strong>dire</strong>: <em>je vois, nous voyons, ils voient</em>; <em>je dis, nous disons, ils disent</em>. Only <em>vous dites</em> and <em>vous faites</em> end in <em>-tes</em>. Not: <em>vous disez, vous faisez</em>.",
        "tag": "voir, dire"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>venir de</strong> says where you come from: <em>Je viens de Dakar. Tu viens d'où ?</em> <strong>prendre</strong> means take, and also have a drink or a meal: <em>Je prends le bus. Je prends un café.</em>",
        "tag": "venir de, prendre"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Je vas</em> → ✓ <em>Je vais</em>. ✗ <em>Vous faisez</em> → ✓ <em>Vous faites</em>. ✗ <em>Ils venent</em> → ✓ <em>Ils viennent</em>. ✗ <em>Il prende</em> → ✓ <em>Il prend</em>. Also learn <a href=\"/etre-avoir\">être and avoir</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
        {
          "caption": "The seven key verbs",
          "head": ["", "aller", "faire", "venir", "prendre", "mettre", "voir", "dire"],
